{:edb/db-before-t 2840 :edb/db-after-t 2841 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536153 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:14:22.493Z" 13194139536153 true] [17592186119514 1100 "Calculate\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081343/assets/15738eec7022be44c90577b931f2e8aa8446a8fc3f911260fcf468ae6ddd2d57.png)" 13194139536153 true] [17592186119514 1100 "Calculate\n$\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}$" 13194139536153 false] [17592186119514 1145 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-8)+(0-0) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081343/assets/ce96c053abb756977f717e7130257af39307e5f663596042ddcb5b14b0153cb8.png)" 13194139536153 true] [17592186119514 1145 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-8)+(0-0) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536153 false] [17592186119515 1100 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081343/assets/82b151cbc7d19f4afa375763d0ca8eb281d76e11f2d775c068e97dbf3d40cae4.png)" 13194139536153 true] [17592186119515 1100 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536153 false] [17592186119515 1145 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081343/assets/efb849ac6e240be4d0a6aac3e7f9405158b8b1bb996956b7346c02b5bee751b1.png)" 13194139536153 true] [17592186119515 1145 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536153 false] [17592186119584 1100 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081343/assets/502b9464378fb5030a72918f45938184f120773e4639f3f50f071ca82fc307ce.png)" 13194139536153 true] [17592186119584 1100 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536153 false] [17592186119584 1145 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-6)\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}